The Nautilus DevOps team is strategizing the migration of a portion of their infrastructure to the AWS cloud. 
As part of this phased migration approach, they need to allocate an Elastic IP address to support external access for specific workloads.

For this task, create an AWS Elastic IP using Terraform with the following requirement:

The Elastic IP name xfusion-eip should be stored in a variable named KKE_eip. The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.
Note:

The configuration values should be stored in a variables.tf file.

The Terraform script should be structured with a main.tf file referencing variables.tf.

Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To satisfy this task, you need two files: variables.tf (holding the configuration value) and main.tf (referencing that variable). Since an Elastic IP doesn't have a native name field, the name is applied via the Name tag.

📄 /home/bob/terraform/variables.tf
hcl
variable "KKE_eip" {
  description = "Name tag for the Elastic IP"
  type        = string
  default     = "xfusion-eip"
}
📄 /home/bob/terraform/main.tf
hcl
resource "aws_eip" "xfusion_eip" {
  tags = {
    Name = var.KKE_eip
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Variable KKE_eip: Holds the Elastic IP name xfusion-eip, exactly as required.

Name tag: The EIP's name in AWS comes from the Name tag, so Name = var.KKE_eip is how the variable is applied.

No hardcoded values in main.tf: All configuration lives in variables.tf, keeping the script properly structured.

Region: Handled by your existing provider.tf (us-east-1), so no provider block is needed here.

No vpc = true: In AWS provider v5, EIPs are VPC-based by default, so that argument is unnecessary (and deprecated).

🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Confirm No Changes
bash
terraform plan
It should return:

text
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
🔍 Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-addresses \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=xfusion-eip" \
  --query "Addresses[].{IP:PublicIp,Name:Tags[?Key=='Name']|[0].Value}"
You should see the Elastic IP with the name xfusion-eip, confirming the variable was applied correctly.
